{"profiles":[{"commonNames":[{"language":"en","name":"Margot Marsh's Mouse Lemur","source":"The IUCN Red List of Threatened Species"},{"language":"en","name":"Antafondro Mouse Lemur","source":"Catalogue of Life"},{"language":"en","name":"Margot Marsh’s Mouse Lemur","source":"Catalogue of Life"}],"gbif":{"canonicalName":"Microcebus margotmarshae","confidence":99,"rank":"species","scientificName":"Microcebus margotmarshae Louis Jr., Engberg, McGuire, McCormick, Randriamampionona, Ranaivoarisoa, Bailey et al., 2008","status":"ACCEPTED","usageKey":8235495},"ottId":5146,"rank":"species","scientificName":"Microcebus margotmarshae","synonyms":[],"wikipedia":{"extract":"Margot Marsh's mouse lemur or the Antafondro mouse lemur (Microcebus margotmarshae) is a species of mouse lemur endemic to Madagascar. Its holotype was first collected on 21 May 2006, proposed in 2006 by Andriantompohavana et al., and was formally described in 2008 by E. Lewis, Jr., et al.","revisionId":1356978097,"title":"Margot Marsh's mouse lemur","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Margot_Marsh%27s_mouse_lemur","wikidataId":"Q865539"}},{"commonNames":[{"language":"en","name":"Red Brown Lemur","source":"The IUCN Red List of Threatened Species"},{"language":"en","name":"Red-fronted Lemur","source":"Catalogue of Life"},{"language":"en","name":"Rufous Brown Lemur","source":"Catalogue of Life"},{"language":"en","name":"Audebert's Brown Lemur","source":"Catalogue of Life"},{"language":"en","name":"Red-fronted Brown Lemur","source":"Catalogue of Life"},{"language":"en","name":"Northern Red-fronted Brown Lemur","source":"Catalogue of Life"}],"gbif":{"canonicalName":"Eulemur rufus","confidence":99,"rank":"species","scientificName":"Eulemur rufus (Audebert, 1799)","status":"ACCEPTED","usageKey":4266934},"ottId":22554,"rank":"species","scientificName":"Eulemur rufus","synonyms":["Eulemur fulvus rufus","Lemur rufus"],"wikipedia":{"extract":"The red lemur (Eulemur rufus), also known as the rufous brown lemur or northern red-fronted lemur, is a species of lemur from Madagascar. Until 2001, the species E. rufus was considered a subspecies of the common brown lemur, E. fulvus, after which it was classified as its own species.","revisionId":1356979492,"title":"Red lemur","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Red_lemur","wikidataId":"Q520434"}},{"commonNames":[{"language":"en","name":"Capped Langur","source":"The IUCN Red List of Threatened Species"},{"language":"en","name":"durga","source":"Catalogue of Life"},{"language":"en","name":"brahma","source":"Catalogue of Life"},{"language":"en","name":"pileatus","source":"Catalogue of Life"},{"language":"en","name":"tenebricus","source":"Catalogue of Life"},{"language":"en","name":"Capped Lutung","source":"Catalogue of Life"}],"gbif":{"canonicalName":"Trachypithecus pileatus","confidence":99,"rank":"species","scientificName":"Trachypithecus pileatus (Blyth, 1843)","status":"ACCEPTED","usageKey":2436538},"ottId":118938,"rank":"species","scientificName":"Trachypithecus pileatus","synonyms":["Macacus pileatus","Semnopithecus pileatus","Trachypithecus argentatus","Trachypithecus saturatus"],"wikipedia":{"extract":"The capped langur (Trachypithecus pileatus) is a primate species in the family Cercopithecidae native to subtropical and tropical dry forests in northeast India, Bhutan, Bangladesh and Myanmar. It is arboreal and feeds on 43 plant species.","revisionId":1356935827,"title":"Capped langur","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Capped_langur","wikidataId":"Q205700"}},{"commonNames":[],"ottId":145562,"scientificName":"Pezoporus","synonyms":[],"wikipedia":{"extract":"Pezoporus is a genus of parrot endemic to Australia. It contains two species: the night parrot (P. occidentalis) and the ground parrot (P. wallicus) which is divided into three subspecies.","revisionId":1315059541,"title":"Pezoporus","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pezoporus","wikidataId":"Q301232"}},{"commonNames":[{"language":"en","name":"Northern Band-tailed Pigeon","source":"The IUCN Red List of Threatened Species"},{"language":"en","name":"Band-tailed Pigeon","source":"Catalogue of Life"},{"language":"en","name":"Band-tailed Pigeon (Northern)","source":"The Clements Checklist"},{"language":"en","name":"Band-tailed Pigeon (White-necked)","source":"The Clements Checklist"}],"gbif":{"canonicalName":"Patagioenas fasciata","confidence":99,"rank":"species","scientificName":"Patagioenas fasciata (Say, 1822)","status":"ACCEPTED","usageKey":2495266},"ottId":148378,"rank":"species","scientificName":"Patagioenas fasciata","synonyms":["Columba fasciata"],"wikipedia":{"extract":"The band-tailed pigeon (Patagioenas fasciata) is a pigeon native to the Americas, and one of the largest pigeons in this region. It is largely gray, but with a thin white collar on the nape and a purple-gray breast.","revisionId":1347933069,"title":"Band-tailed pigeon","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Band-tailed_pigeon","wikidataId":"Q512061"}},{"commonNames":[{"language":"en","name":"Falcons","source":"Dyntaxa. Svensk taxonomisk databas"},{"language":"en","name":"Falcons And Caracaras","source":"Checklist of Vermont Species"},{"language":"en","name":"Caracaras","source":"Catalogue of Life"},{"language":"en","name":"Caracaras, Falcons","source":"Checklist of Vermont Species"},{"language":"en","name":"Falcons, Caracaras","source":"Martha's Vineyard species checklist"}],"gbif":{"canonicalName":"Falconidae","confidence":94,"rank":"family","scientificName":"Falconidae","status":"ACCEPTED","usageKey":5240},"ottId":212186,"rank":"family","scientificName":"Falconidae","synonyms":[],"wikipedia":{"extract":"The falcons and caracaras are around 65 species of diurnal birds of prey that make up the family Falconidae (representing all extant species in the order Falconiformes). The family likely originated in South America during the Paleocene and is divided into three subfamilies: Herpetotherinae, which includes the laughing falcon and the forest falcons; Polyborinae, which includes the spot-winged falconet and the caracaras; and Falconinae, the true falcons, hobbies and kestrels (Falco) and the falconets (Microhierax).","revisionId":1354727030,"title":"Falconidae","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Falconidae","wikidataId":"Q21744"}},{"commonNames":[{"language":"en","name":"Tropicbirds","source":"Catalogue of Life"}],"gbif":{"canonicalName":"Phaethon","confidence":94,"rank":"genus","scientificName":"Phaethon Linnaeus, 1758","status":"ACCEPTED","usageKey":2481360},"ottId":330010,"rank":"genus","scientificName":"Phaethon","synonyms":["Lepturus","Phaeton","Pheton"]},{"commonNames":[{"language":"en","name":"Adzebills","source":"Catalogue of Life"}],"gbif":{"canonicalName":"Aptornis","confidence":94,"rank":"genus","scientificName":"Aptornis Owen, 1848","status":"ACCEPTED","usageKey":9528154},"ottId":440602,"rank":"genus","scientificName":"Aptornis","synonyms":["Apterornis"],"wikipedia":{"extract":"The adzebills are two species of gruiform birds belonging to the genus Aptornis, the sole member of the extinct family Aptornithidae, which were endemic to New Zealand. The species were divided between the North Island and South Island of the country, with Aptornis otidiformis being the North Island adzebill, and Aptornis defossor being the South Island adzebill.","revisionId":1373050294,"title":"Adzebill","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Adzebill","wikidataId":"Q144809"}},{"commonNames":[{"language":"en","name":"Gray Langurs","source":"Catalogue of Life"},{"language":"en","name":"Indian langurs","source":"Catalogue of Life"},{"language":"en","name":"Gray and Nilgiri Langurs","source":"Catalogue of Life"},{"language":"en","name":"Grey and Nilgiri Langurs","source":"Catalogue of Life"},{"language":"en","name":"Hanuman and Nilgiri Langurs","source":"Catalogue of Life"}],"gbif":{"canonicalName":"Semnopithecus","confidence":94,"rank":"genus","scientificName":"Semnopithecus Desmarest, 1822","status":"ACCEPTED","usageKey":2436563},"ottId":547162,"rank":"genus","scientificName":"Semnopithecus","synonyms":[],"wikipedia":{"extract":"Semnopithecus is a genus of Old World monkeys native to the Indian subcontinent, with all species with the exception of two being commonly known as gray langurs. Traditionally only the species Semnopithecus entellus was recognized, but since about 2001 additional species have been recognized.","revisionId":1348413446,"title":"Semnopithecus","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Semnopithecus","wikidataId":"Q721407"}},{"commonNames":[{"language":"en","name":"Bale Monkey","source":"The IUCN Red List of Threatened Species"},{"language":"en","name":"Djam-djam","source":"Catalogue of Life"},{"language":"en","name":"Bale Mountains Grivet","source":"Catalogue of Life"},{"language":"en","name":"Bale Mountains Monkey","source":"Catalogue of Life"},{"language":"en","name":"Bale Mountains Vervet","source":"Catalogue of Life"}],"gbif":{"canonicalName":"Chlorocebus djamdjamensis","confidence":99,"rank":"species","scientificName":"Chlorocebus djamdjamensis (Neumann, 1902)","status":"ACCEPTED","usageKey":4267124},"ottId":875546,"rank":"species","scientificName":"Chlorocebus djamdjamensis","synonyms":["Cercopithecus aethiops djamdjamensis","Cercopithecus djamdjamensis"],"wikipedia":{"extract":"The Bale Mountains vervet (Chlorocebus djamdjamensis) is a terrestrial Old World monkey endemic to Ethiopia, found in the bamboo forests of the Bale Mountains. All species in Chlorocebus were formerly in the genus Cercopithecus.","revisionId":1313869796,"title":"Bale Mountains vervet","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bale_Mountains_vervet","wikidataId":"Q804876"}},{"commonNames":[{"language":"en","name":"Melanesian Bronzewings","source":"Catalogue of Life"},{"language":"en","name":"New Guinean Bronzewings","source":"Catalogue of Life"}],"gbif":{"canonicalName":"Henicophaps","confidence":94,"rank":"genus","scientificName":"Henicophaps G.R.Gray, 1862","status":"ACCEPTED","usageKey":2495830},"ottId":956250,"rank":"genus","scientificName":"Henicophaps","synonyms":[],"wikipedia":{"extract":"Henicophaps is a small genus of doves that are endemic to New Guinea and the Bismarck Archipelago. These are stocky pigeons with unusually long heavy bills that live in wet forests and forage primary on the ground.","revisionId":1223563924,"title":"Henicophaps","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Henicophaps","wikidataId":"Q168384"}},{"commonNames":[{"language":"en","name":"zebra danio","source":"Catalogue of Life"}],"gbif":{"canonicalName":"Danio rerio","confidence":97,"rank":"species","scientificName":"Danio rerio (Hamilton, 1822)","status":"ACCEPTED","usageKey":9797255},"ottId":1005914,"rank":"species","scientificName":"Danio rerio","synonyms":["Barilius rerio","Brachydanio frankei","Brachydanio rerio","Cyprinus chapalio","Cyprinus rerio","Danio frankei","Danio lineatus","Nuria rerio"],"wikipedia":{"extract":"The zebrafish (Danio rerio) is a species of danionin belonging to the genus Danio in the suborder Cyprinoidei. Native to South Asia, it is a popular, hardy aquarium fish, frequently sold under the trade name zebra danio (thus often mislabeled as a \"tropical fish\", even though they also inhabit subtropical and temperate waters).","revisionId":1374724903,"title":"Zebrafish","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zebrafish","wikidataId":"Q169444"}},{"commonNames":[{"language":"en","name":"Ahmanson’s Sportive Lemur","source":"Lepilemuridae"},{"language":"en","name":"Tsiombikibo Sportive Lemur","source":"Integrated Taxonomic Information System (ITIS)"},{"language":"en","name":"Ahmanson's sportive lemur","source":"Wikipedia"}],"gbif":{"canonicalName":"Lepilemur ahmansonorum","confidence":98,"rank":"species","scientificName":"Lepilemur ahmansonorum Louis Jr., Engberg, Lei, Geng, Sommer, Randriamampionona, Randriamanana, Zaonarivelo et al., 2006","status":"SYNONYM","usageKey":5786416},"ottId":3607514,"rank":"species","scientificName":"Lepilemur ahmansonorum","synonyms":[],"wikipe